With the same change made to `fesm2022/tree.mjs` of `@angular/cdk` 22.2.5, whose `tree.ts` is the same as 22.3.0's, every row of the reproduction had the same `aria-posinset` and `aria-setsize` as before, and a click with 40,000 children took 9 ms (the table below).

Until a release has it, a directive that extends `MatTreeNode`, with its selector and providing `CdkTreeNode`, can override `_getPositionInSet()` to look the position up in a map of its own. That is how we work around it, though it relies on an underscored method.

Possibly related, not measured: with a `levelAccessor`, `_calculateParents()` finds each node's parent with `_findParentForNode()`, which walks back over the node's preceding siblings, so computing the rendering data is quadratic in a set as well, though only when the data or the expansion changes.

### Reproduction

StackBlitz link: https://stackblitz.com/edit/ordthymq
Steps to reproduce:
1. Open the StackBlitz. The tree has one parent with 10,000 children; `?children=40000` in the preview's URL changes how many.
2. Click "Toggle" to expand the parent.
3. Click a child, or press ArrowDown in the tree.
4. The console shows how long each click and key took, from the event to the frame after it.

The project is the docs' StackBlitz starter (22.3.0), with this file as `src/example/material-docs-example.ts`:

<details>
<summary><code>src/example/material-docs-example.ts</code></summary>

```ts
import {Component} from '@angular/core';
import {MatTreeModule} from '@angular/material/tree';

interface Item {
  name: string;
  children?: Item[];
}

/** How many children the one parent has: 10,000, or what `?children=` in the page's URL says. */
const childCount = Number(new URLSearchParams(location.search).get('children') ?? 10_000);

const parent: Item = {
  name: `Parent of ${childCount} children`,
  children: Array.from({length: childCount}, (_, i) => ({name: `Child ${i + 1}`})),
};

/**
 * A tree with one parent of many children. Expand the parent, then click a child or press ArrowDown: the console says
 * how long each click and key took, from the event to the frame after it.
 */
@Component({
  selector: 'material-docs-example',
  imports: [MatTreeModule],
  template: `
    <mat-tree [dataSource]="dataSource" [childrenAccessor]="childrenAccessor">
      <mat-tree-node *matTreeNodeDef="let item">{{ item.name }}</mat-tree-node>
      <mat-tree-node *matTreeNodeDef="let item; when: hasChildren" isExpandable>
        <button matTreeNodeToggle>Toggle</button>
        {{ item.name }}
      </mat-tree-node>
    </mat-tree>
  `,
})
export class MaterialDocsExample {
  readonly dataSource = [parent];
  readonly childrenAccessor = (item: Item) => item.children ?? [];
  readonly hasChildren = (_: number, item: Item) => !!item.children?.length;
}

// Logs how long each click and key took, from the event (before Angular's listeners) to the frame after it.
for (const type of ['click', 'keydown']) {
  document.addEventListener(
    type,
    () => {
      const start = performance.now();
      requestAnimationFrame(() =>
        setTimeout(() => console.log(`${type}: ${Math.round(performance.now() - start)} ms`)),
      );
    },
    {capture: true},
  );
}
```

